#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064038 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2bb17470-6365-5e08-b649-f9f42146a4c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-732" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 2\\sin (3x - 1)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function\n\n$$\nf = 2\\sin u, \\qquad u=3x-1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 2\\cos u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= 2\\cos u \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 6\\cos u \\\\\n&= 6\\cos (3x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064039 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f56b401-c800-5708-8978-b84ce3f542f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-733" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ if $y = 4\\csc (5x + 3)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 4 \\csc u, \\qquad u = 5x+3\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -4\\csc u\\cot u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&=-4\\csc u\\cot u \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&=-20\\csc u\\cot u \\\\\n&=-20\\csc (5x + 3)\\cot (5x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "373a63fc-3dae-5880-8a49-63b5c64888f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-734" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\sin^3{x}$, calculate $f'(x)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = (\\sin{x})^3\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = u^3, \\qquad u=\\sin x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 3u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{\\prime }(x) &= \\frac{df}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= 3u^{2} \\cdot (\\cos x) \\\\\n&= 3(\\sin x)^{2}\\cos x \\\\\n&= 3\\sin^{2} x\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064042 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ebd0289-b278-5352-b7bd-10ae218c43fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-735" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y = \\tan{\\left(\\sin(x)\\right)}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan u, \\qquad u = \\sin x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec^2 u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\sec^{2} u \\cdot \\cos x \\\\\n&= \\sec^{2} (\\sin x)\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82659cf2-0d0d-571c-b677-a9734bdbc77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12x^3}{\\cos(3x^4-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249853} {:answer/id #uuid "ec035f43-416c-54c4-bdd9-7691e264d439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12x^3}{\\cos(3x^4-5)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec035f43-416c-54c4-bdd9-7691e264d439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12x^3}{\\cos(3x^4-5)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249854} :answer-field/id #uuid "99c14107-35e5-52c8-83bc-1bf1bfa7db9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249852}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8cfab55f-c242-579e-bda0-8364a79dbcb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-310179" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3{x}^{4}-5)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡u,\\,u=3{x}^{4}-5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={sec}^{2}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=12{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot 12{x}^{3} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}{sec}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}{sec}^{2}⁡(3{x}^{4}-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5937a9f-fc76-5f26-945c-920b1984b07e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249857} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5e87b6-f630-5904-b169-dff4595b1ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d5e87b6-f630-5904-b169-dff4595b1ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249858} :answer-field/id #uuid "16a10934-9000-5467-9780-1c375d10c554" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249856}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6e54a0b-e178-5c73-9eda-94e5c3fac631" :question/math-academy-id "q-310180" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is given by ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is \n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95b67c40-7340-5f04-8aea-22ad839d66c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-6x^2}{\\sin(2x^3)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95b67c40-7340-5f04-8aea-22ad839d66c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-6x^2}{\\sin(2x^3)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249861} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7690651-f37f-597a-8dc6-f34c249d582f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249860}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c90cfa9-0486-5203-9da6-c4a1b9ecc483" :question/math-academy-id "q-310222" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2{x}^{3}),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot 6{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6{x}^{2}{csc}^{2}⁡(2{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76fd204e-60e7-5ff5-9218-21d79e5b7a9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249864} {:answer/id #uuid "3f17b977-aa51-5d8b-aee8-90db30dbda49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249865} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc70b98-d298-59c7-938c-cc7c651b0ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249866} {:answer/id #uuid "36261555-c139-5597-a434-0b8bd0c91c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249867} {:answer/id #uuid "c05fb58d-9abe-58c1-af56-6ca076f3b64a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dc70b98-d298-59c7-938c-cc7c651b0ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249866} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fe8829f-3848-511c-aeb8-9a5d23803897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249863}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e330e01e-de68-56a1-a720-c5093ac56875" :question/math-academy-id "q-31153" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)$ at the point where $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is given by ${f}^{′}(2).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=3x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 2+5) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡11.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c97683d2-8e12-524c-8411-b23e88ede341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11-2x)-9{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249871} {:answer/id #uuid "12305e26-6a25-5c6d-810e-b9d752269023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11-2x)-18{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249872} {:answer/id #uuid "476c6d00-be94-525a-894a-65cbdc6739c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11-2x)-9{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249873} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7028e6-fa2e-574b-bc01-412f1172e3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11-2x)-18{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249874} {:answer/id #uuid "a445d0d3-e28c-5b06-91d1-143b05f19bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11-2x)-18{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c7028e6-fa2e-574b-bc01-412f1172e3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11-2x)-18{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249874} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b8fb54-5311-5364-bd5c-960c7033a62f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249870}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "893bef04-f620-5797-a1c9-55f06c3838d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-31163" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11-2x)-6{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11-2x)$ and $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x)+h(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11-2x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=11-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11-2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-18{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)+{h}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11-2x)-18{x}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfd75089-4674-5c53-8373-d731852c4d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249878} {:answer/id #uuid "019c222f-c9f3-5a25-a904-8a8c25580cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249879} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf2b083-1471-502a-ab2d-d78f65d2cc16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249880} {:answer/id #uuid "79742951-461d-59e5-b363-1f07b5ba4759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249881} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7c88df-be7b-58ec-bcd7-31051e196f1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "019c222f-c9f3-5a25-a904-8a8c25580cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249879} :answer-field/id #uuid "354b7fde-3028-5afe-b2b4-c24851f21ad4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249877}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62c04832-86fc-5442-be8e-193f44e663b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-31320" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sec}⁡u,\\,u=2x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "601da557-6c68-57ab-82f9-a0704a025da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249885} {:answer/id #uuid "5564dadc-9793-5184-8969-31e9829a0d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249886} {:answer/id #uuid "c42c4972-fb0a-5aa5-973d-ae1f029e11d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249887} {:answer/id #uuid "7b01cb61-da98-5256-a277-93edab9727ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249888} {:answer/id #uuid "5b180426-2802-55de-befa-7a0a05429e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "601da557-6c68-57ab-82f9-a0704a025da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249885} :answer-field/id #uuid "f28b8fa5-063f-566c-87c0-d65d3defd467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249884}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0a20154-4979-52d5-a59b-8c443685f700" :question/math-academy-id "q-31338" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\pi -3x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is given by ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2\\pi -3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3faac535-1eed-5d9d-8b81-042bd18237dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249892} {:answer/id #uuid "993d6b2a-cc06-5faa-93ff-5518d0789830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249893} {:answer/id #uuid "ba3275da-ff1a-5649-82ea-ac7d71f6d280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249894} {:answer/id #uuid "4fccd886-915b-581e-b60e-103bcac02004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249895} {:answer/id #uuid "c26813cd-bc60-5821-b9bf-ac72705909cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba3275da-ff1a-5649-82ea-ac7d71f6d280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249894} :answer-field/id #uuid "e567891c-e76a-566c-8cc5-e92026da2c00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249891}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20ad52c2-8f54-5714-be4e-62fde8b2ebfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-31340" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x)$ at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{csc}⁡u,\\,u=5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=-\\pi /2} & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x){|}_{x=-\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "858dc101-b0d5-5de1-a73d-7a42c8fb0b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249899} {:answer/id #uuid "79b35e0d-fade-5801-8857-5ae0d019c226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79b35e0d-fade-5801-8857-5ae0d019c226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249900} :answer-field/id #uuid "153a3731-b23c-5cde-bede-9b86e334f429" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95007ef1-5230-5101-b725-5c0d1b012c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95007ef1-5230-5101-b725-5c0d1b012c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249902} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cc64189-cabc-5ea6-bcad-88bfb32a1865" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249901}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "395980ab-fd4b-5bbb-9601-2d0aca096ab9" :question/math-academy-id "q-341223" :question/problem "Given that $y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef1fc1c7-3c78-540f-8701-969e6482754d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sin(x)^6\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249905} {:answer/id #uuid "a6045304-3b9d-5a26-be4c-5749b64dbf6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sin(x)^6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef1fc1c7-3c78-540f-8701-969e6482754d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sin(x)^6\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249905} :answer-field/id #uuid "502e5783-ff77-5ac7-8ba4-e1a9750377b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249904}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ae12492-8df1-5ee2-8dab-82cebc31f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-342420" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={sin}^{7}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{7},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=7{u}^{6},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =7{u}^{6}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =7{sin}^{6}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aed239e-8e9e-5d86-8e94-114d42429c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20\\cos(x)^4\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249909} {:answer/id #uuid "8af6499f-b8c4-507f-92d1-c7b735b28355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\cos(x)^4\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8af6499f-b8c4-507f-92d1-c7b735b28355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\cos(x)^4\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249910} :answer-field/id #uuid "16edfbcd-48e6-56d9-944c-70c5ca3c214a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249908}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41618891-0d89-52d2-8e35-cd9776cd53cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-342424" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-4{cos}^{5}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))^{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-4{u}^{5},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-20{u}^{4},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-20{u}^{4}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =20{cos}^{4}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0770d101-f764-5cd4-8e13-9f5c8482a895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\cos(\\tan(x))^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0770d101-f764-5cd4-8e13-9f5c8482a895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\cos(\\tan(x))^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249913} :answer-field/id #uuid "2418653b-858c-5b51-927a-a4714e73acfa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249912}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6814a75-da83-5355-bf98-1eb781c96296" :question/math-academy-id "q-342436" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={sec}^{2}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63cff444-0b6a-5785-b3e7-e80a2462d296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\csc(x)\\cot(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63cff444-0b6a-5785-b3e7-e80a2462d296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\csc(x)\\cot(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249916} :answer-field/id #uuid "8edd6508-9cc8-5b4a-9d19-386929612f2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249915} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c77d6cae-5d31-53c6-b01c-d9ed1ce04432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\csc(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c77d6cae-5d31-53c6-b01c-d9ed1ce04432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\csc(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249918} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b99f02-cbb9-502f-85d4-d98647f81525" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249917}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a324772-0fed-5cd0-8f75-f83474598907" :question/math-academy-id "q-342445" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73e0aed9-9715-5790-9947-4275f50e7dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249921} {:answer/id #uuid "c95daef1-8352-537c-bf99-0fa1f93a1e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249922} {:answer/id #uuid "2a470438-fff3-5dfc-9fa0-6324ba0c0734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249923} {:answer/id #uuid "b053f24c-4996-5ce9-98d0-dc47c9aea36e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249924} {:answer/id #uuid "7f81fd48-a200-5815-8e5a-8f081ffac6ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a470438-fff3-5dfc-9fa0-6324ba0c0734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249923} :answer-field/id #uuid "58eac446-51d4-59e2-bf70-de88799bc63e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249920}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4144dd65-7226-52bc-a7d8-5957ea70967a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48512" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\pi -3x)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is given by ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2\\pi -3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(-{csc}^{2}⁡u)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =3(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =3x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5d0ac51-4c7b-54b8-89bd-6faf18852a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249928} {:answer/id #uuid "dfaf06f9-80d2-59fa-863e-5d357a9127ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249929} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a330b5-009a-52f4-b367-473bbfb1b19a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249930} {:answer/id #uuid "9c693f85-719c-5df5-8530-abf66c5f3571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249931} {:answer/id #uuid "6c027a30-dd2d-5e37-973b-33f1fddabd68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c693f85-719c-5df5-8530-abf66c5f3571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249931} :answer-field/id #uuid "cabeb49d-b84f-526d-b429-420f757cd5a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249927}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90cda8c9-8228-53b9-bb6f-76648ef22cc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-48560" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=2u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab496644-0eea-5183-a453-56693656c987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cos x\\sin (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063006} {:answer/id #uuid "af5114a0-ffc1-5411-bb8f-c0d496caf27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cos x\\cos (\\sin x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063007} {:answer/id #uuid "c71e2405-337a-57ba-87b2-17386379688e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin x\\cos (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063008} {:answer/id #uuid "eb27c530-bb19-5883-b23d-768448bf54ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin x\\cos (\\sin x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063009} {:answer/id #uuid "45bc7f9f-8d02-50ed-9a1f-42e81aa8fd39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin x\\sin (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c71e2405-337a-57ba-87b2-17386379688e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin x\\cos (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063008} :answer-field/id #uuid "388f85b8-8e2a-5fe4-abe4-21648326a68c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063005}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55ba3ddf-3b81-51ce-a46b-d6facbbbea67" :question/math-academy-id "q-48643" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y = \\sin (\\cos x)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The outer function is sine and the inner function is $u=\\cos x$. Hence\n$$y'=\\cos(u)u'=\\cos(\\cos x)(-\\sin x)=-\\sin x\\cos(\\cos x).$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d73f56b1-72e9-57a6-8bcc-057cd05ae84f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-\\pi -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249935} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d23b72-d731-5471-b74f-b9ad4bb4648c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249936} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6d7c03-57ab-5d89-83fb-8d4d2fc62d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249937} {:answer/id #uuid "03a18169-8bbb-5b45-8963-cdb629b604d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249938} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b57ff7-acb7-5f51-9f0e-e35a8da46dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0b57ff7-acb7-5f51-9f0e-e35a8da46dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249939} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d65b6f-1e64-5150-a3a7-70e1c80995c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249934}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dac57425-439e-57f2-8c4b-329068ff1544" :question/math-academy-id "q-51026" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)$ at the point $(1+\\pi ,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=1+\\pi $ is given by ${f}^{′}(1+\\pi ).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1+\\pi ,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1+\\pi ) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1+\\pi -1) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(1+\\pi ,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =0(x-(1+\\pi )) \\\\ y+2 & =0 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "957fe26b-ac75-54a9-9e2a-0ec7f3a0d2bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\sin x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063659} {:answer/id #uuid "f4cac8b6-1af2-5e7c-8d04-169a5d6502d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\sin x\\cos^{2} x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063660} {:answer/id #uuid "17ddd4cf-50b0-5098-9504-054608cbc91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063661} {:answer/id #uuid "451f68f1-197c-595e-9429-385e13e286b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\cos^{2} x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063662} {:answer/id #uuid "775ccd6a-dabd-591a-90a8-b4db265276aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\sin x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063663} {:answer/id #uuid "15f7bd42-8327-5e09-a6fb-56eefbef89da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249940} {:answer/id #uuid "70082d94-4345-507c-9e3d-0cdd4ed3e012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "775ccd6a-dabd-591a-90a8-b4db265276aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\sin x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063663} :answer-field/id #uuid "4059c0e9-ff43-5297-92ae-c3a09f60138d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063658}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "270b54b6-fdc6-5a75-af7c-f8052eee38ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-72154" :question/problem "Find $f^{\\prime }(x)$ when $f(x) = 9\\sin^{2} (x)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Write $f(x)=9(\\sin x)^2$. The chain rule gives\n$$f'(x)=9\\cdot2\\sin x\\cdot\\cos x=18\\sin x\\cos x.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4995489-1af9-5779-a344-a958925a6cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{sin}^{7}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249944} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1fdb45-bad5-54a7-b9e7-4b21dff7406b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{7}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249945} {:answer/id #uuid "3d33837b-4c69-54cb-9b6d-d761eb8463b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40{cos}^{7}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249946} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce228d4-4fa3-5766-bf52-775d47061ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{7}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249947} {:answer/id #uuid "b6705871-38da-5e55-a4be-03e6e35a8e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40{cos}^{7}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d33837b-4c69-54cb-9b6d-d761eb8463b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40{cos}^{7}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249946} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc3a383-3cd2-5cef-b0d6-bdf8180d64d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249943}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05c5e383-09df-5a39-ad1a-77ea88b63ed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-72156" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)=5{cos}^{8}⁡(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=5{u}^{8},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=40{u}^{7},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =40{u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =40(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{7}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-40{cos}^{7}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0c68edb-5213-50eb-b467-de5306b2c6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249951} {:answer/id #uuid "783d43e6-aa55-5357-8c5c-6216e9dfba91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249952} {:answer/id #uuid "8acd60a1-93b3-55b9-a976-f21972ee8df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249953} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cc3570-7b46-5bdf-8eca-1d1d299e114d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249954} {:answer/id #uuid "66f5e1bd-da78-53b0-b7c2-000c9064f900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7cc3570-7b46-5bdf-8eca-1d1d299e114d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249954} :answer-field/id #uuid "8499a557-a87c-542e-9f5c-37d383c8727f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249950}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0850c6d8-9345-5a32-9826-232b712af5ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-72173" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡u,\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
